The needed change is in the app’s paste and media-rendering code: preserve the animated bytes when pasting, then render the GIF as a normal inline <img> instead of a still thumbnail with a tap-to-open overlay.

// 1. On paste: prefer the animated clipboard item and preserve its bytes.
function handlePaste(event: React.ClipboardEvent) {
  const items = Array.from(event.clipboardData.items);
  const item =
    items.find((item) => item.type === "image/gif") ??
    items.find((item) => item.type === "image/webp") ??
    items.find((item) => item.type.startsWith("image/"));
  const file = item?.getAsFile();
  if (file) uploadOriginalFile(file); // Don't convert it through canvas to PNG.
}
// 2. In the message: render animated formats directly, without a click overlay.
function InlineMedia({ src, mimeType, alt }: {
  src: string;
  mimeType: string;
  alt: string;
}) {
  if (mimeType === "image/gif" || mimeType === "image/webp") {
    return <img className="inline-animation" src={src} alt={alt} />;
  }
  return <ImagePreview src={src} alt={alt} />;
}

The important part is preserving the original GIF through paste and upload. If iOS gives the app only a PNG frame, the lost animation can’t be reconstructed from that frame; the app needs to receive the original GIF data or URL. I can’t install this change into ChatGPT from our conversation, but this is the client-side fix that would make inline GIF playback work.